> In the past, I've told you about the problems with my solar installation, from Green Home Systems. I sent a registered and certified letter to the corporate address on their website, and got the letter back with "forward time exp  rtn to send", and a different address from the post office. Do I try this now? Or do I get in touch with a lawyer now?
**assistant**:
> Do both, in parallel.
> 
> - **Re-send immediately** to the forwarding address, and also to every notice address/email in the contract. Keep the unopened returned envelope, tracking, copies, and photos.
> - **Check Minnesota Secretary of State records** for GHS’s registered agent and current business address; send the notice there too. A lawyer can do this quickly if the entity name is unclear.
> - Send by **certified mail (return receipt requested)** and a trackable courier; also email a PDF the same day. Delivery/refusal/failed forwarding can all help establish notice.
> - Given the year-plus delay and apparent address instability, **consult a Minnesota construction/consumer-contract lawyer now**, rather than waiting for another missed deadline. You need advice before terminating, hiring someone else, withholding/payment disputes, or making a formal damages demand.
> - Do not sign a cancellation, release, change order, or new financing document without review.
> 
> Your prior letter is still useful evidence: it shows a good-faith effort to give formal notice at the address GHS publicly represented as its corporate address.
